WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:10.450
So, what is the point of sex?

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.910
I'm sure that some, if not all of you have
some ideas.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:22.100
Maybe you’re thinking up a number of original, ingenious witticisms to answer my question with.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.100
And why not?

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:31.100
In the millennia upon millennia of human sexuality,
the act itself has become no less fascinating

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:33.570
or complicated or fraught.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.970
It has shaped our societies and informed our
art.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:43.079
And yes, it can also be absurd and it can
make you laugh.

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:49.670
We made a whole episode about tardigrade sex
and there were a non-zero number of puns.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:55.630
While not all sex is reproductive, today,
we’re going to focus on sexual reproduction.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:00.850
What's important to remember in that context
is that sexual reproduction is not just some

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:07.320
mechanical process between individuals, it
is also cellular.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:14.170
And while that can get lost in the mating
habits of metazoans, protozoans--or single-celled

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:21.630
eukaryotes--contain many of the keys to understanding
how sexual reproduction came to be.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:28.940
Cuz it’s important to remember that sex
was an evolutionary innovation, a thing that at

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:36.270
one point, did not happen and then began to happen, and the advantages of that caused

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:42.220
sexual reproduction to be carried over throughout
the eons up until today.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:55.780
But the evolutionary history of sexual reproduction
is complicated and it is mysterious.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:02.790
And for an illustration of why that is the
case, we're going to turn to amoebas, those

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:07.549
seemingly simple and sexless eukaryotic blobs.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:13.400
Because, it turns out that amoebas aren't
actually that simple.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.940
Or even that sexless.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:24.200
Amoebas are single-celled eukaryotes, united
by the use of pseudopodia (or "false feet")

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.120
that they use to move around and eat.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.840
They're distributed around various eukaryotic
lineages, and we've talked about just how

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:38.980
tangled the taxonomic classification of amoebas
has become in a previous episode.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:45.450
And for a large part of our scientific understanding
and our popular imagination, amoebas have

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.930
been decidedly asexual.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:55.930
In 1932, The New Yorker published a poem called
"Ode to the Amoeba" composed by Arthur Guiterman,

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.569
a writer known for his humor.

00:02:57.569 --> 00:02:59.340
In the poem, he writes:

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.600
Now, there are a lot of scientific details
in there that we could nitpick.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:38.159
But Guiterman's poem helpfully and delightfully
lays out what has long been the pervading

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:45.810
image of amoebas: an ancient being that has
sustained its own individual identity by dividing

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.670
into clonal daughter cells.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:55.349
Those daughter cells continue the process,
propagating into other identical amoebas or

00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:59.670
serving as the evolutionary basis for other
species.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:06.430
That image of the amoeba as primitive organism
is itself linked to what seems like an equally

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:10.700
primitive process: asexual reproduction.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:17.269
After all, if the existential goal of any
organism is to propagate their genetic information,

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:19.930
then division seems simple.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:28.639
It's fast, convenient, and your new clones
should contain 100% of your genome.

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:32.080
But there's a pretty big evolutionary limitation.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:38.099
With more and more divisions, an asexual organism
will start to rack up harmful mutations that

00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:43.499
will eventually kill off the species, this
process has a name.

00:04:43.499 --> 00:04:50.479
It’s called Muller's ratchet and one way
to avoid this outcome is to take in foreign

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:57.839
DNA, a strategy that bacteria and archaea
pursue in their own recombination processes.

00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:02.590
Eukaryotes, as we'll talk about, have sex.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:07.889
Muller's ratchet poses a conundrum for our
understanding of amoebas.

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:15.469
If the theory holds, then any asexual lineage
of organisms shouldn't last very long.

00:05:15.469 --> 00:05:21.520
So how do we square that with the image of
amoebas as an ancient line of organisms?

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.330
The answer is simple: we weren’t wrong about
Muller’s ratchet.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.740
We were wrong about amoebas.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:40.760
Let's start by defining sexual reproduction
at the cellular level, turning to a 2011 paper

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.040
with the wonderful title:

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:55.919
In this work, the authors define sex as "the
presence of a meiotic reduction of the genome

00:05:55.919 --> 00:06:00.300
complement followed eventually by karyogamy."

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.679
That is, ya know, for us, not the set of words
that we might use to describe sex.

00:06:05.679 --> 00:06:14.710
What it means is that sexual reproduction
involves two key processes: meiosis and nuclear fusion.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:20.729
In meiosis, a cell divides, distributing its
genetic material between its progeny cells

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:25.819
and reducing the number of chromosome sets
each cell contains.

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:30.830
After those progeny cells find their mate,
the next step is karyogamy.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:38.520
In this process, the cells fuse all the way
down to their nucleus, thus increasing the

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:45.360
number of chromosome sets and providing the
basis for a whole new member of the family.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:51.179
In plants and animals, that new cell will
then divide and divide and develop into a

00:06:51.179 --> 00:06:53.610
multicellular organism.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:58.339
But in protozoans, that new cell is the organism.

00:06:58.339 --> 00:07:04.490
Like the recombination methods employed by
bacteria, sexual reproduction provides a mechanism

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:08.830
for genetic variability, avoiding Muller's
ratchet.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:15.649
But think of how complicated it must have
been to evolve this process, which is really

00:07:15.649 --> 00:07:18.479
the culmination of several processes.

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:24.439
That altering of the number of chromosomes,
the meiosis, the nuclear fusion--not to mention

00:07:24.439 --> 00:07:28.309
the identification of which organism to mate
with.

00:07:28.309 --> 00:07:35.429
Meiosis itself is so complex that it is hypothesized
to have evolved a single time in the history

00:07:35.429 --> 00:07:37.120
of life on earth.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:43.289
Based on genetic evidence, that last eukaryotic
common ancestor--that mysterious evolutionary

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:51.020
predecessor to amoebas and all other eukaryotes--relied
on sexual reproduction.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:57.540
So if we know that amoebas must be descended
from a sexual microbe, and that the odds are

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:05.400
low on asexual amoebas lasting for long, then
why has this image of the asexual amoeba persisted

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.449
for so long?

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:13.219
This misunderstanding is a product of our
own observational challenges when it comes

00:08:13.219 --> 00:08:14.219
to microbes.

00:08:14.219 --> 00:08:22.429
The fact is that the gold standard in confirming
sex is the ability to actually catch the organisms

00:08:22.429 --> 00:08:23.429
in the act.

00:08:23.429 --> 00:08:28.759
But that poses some clear problems with amoebas
and some other microbes.

00:08:28.759 --> 00:08:32.029
First, and most obvious: they are tiny.

00:08:32.029 --> 00:08:37.039
They can also be difficult to cultivate in
lab conditions, particularly in circumstances

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:38.969
that might lead to sex.

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:45.140
The slime mold Dictyostelium, for example,
was thought to be asexual until the 1970s,

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:51.089
when the scientists realized their samples
were just waiting for the right mate to show up.

00:08:51.089 --> 00:08:56.660
Now, aside from direct observation, we now
have developed genetic methods to determine

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.740
whether an organism might engage in sexual
reproduction.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:06.940
This was the case for Cochliopodium, an amoeba
that was long considered parasexual because

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:11.529
it followed some of the steps required for
sexual reproduction, but it had never been

00:09:11.529 --> 00:09:14.389
observed going through meiosis.

00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:21.959
Instead, in cochliopodium, multiple cells
fuse together to make a big cell with several

00:09:21.959 --> 00:09:24.579
polyploid nuclei.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.860
This mass then breaks up to create cells with
only one nuclei.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:37.019
So just imagine six people fused together
into a ball of goo and then once again break

00:09:37.019 --> 00:09:44.459
apart into six more individual people, now
all the children of those original six.

00:09:44.459 --> 00:09:50.699
It’s a little more complicated than the
way we do it, but I’m not here to judge.

00:09:50.699 --> 00:09:57.260
But in studying Cochliopodium further, researchers
found that they do have meiosis genes, meaning

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:03.470
they probably do reproduce sexually, we’ve
just never been able to see it yet.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:08.360
Scientists have also found extensive genetic
evidence of sex in the Amoebozoa group of

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:12.890
amoebas, further challenging the assumption
that amoebas are asexual.

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:16.649
And this introduces a strange challenge.

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:23.290
We can't assume that just because we haven't
observed any particular species having sex

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:29.139
that it must therefore be strictly asexual
because that’s clearly not the case.

00:10:29.139 --> 00:10:36.540
But, at the same time, we cannot assume that
all eukaryotic life engages in sexual reproduction

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:43.060
because to do so would mean blinding ourselves
to other evolutionary innovations to avoid

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.120
Muller's ratchet.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:50.920
If there are strictly asexual amoebas, then
that's a trait they evolved further away from

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:58.269
their ancestors, perhaps finding an alternative
to sex that can negate some of its downsides,

00:10:58.269 --> 00:11:03.449
like needing to find a good mate or risking
the transmission of disease.

00:11:03.449 --> 00:11:05.990
And they wouldn't be alone.

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:10.870
Bdelloid rotifers are multicellular, and also
asexual.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:16.470
Instead of relying on sexual reproduction
for recombination, they can acquire foreign

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:23.430
DNA during their resting anhydrobiosis state,
which is wild!

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:28.950
Some scientists have hypothesized that asexual
amoeba might have their own strategies, like

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:34.750
relying on having many many chromosomes to
reduce the harm of any mutation.

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:38.960
So what’s the point of sex?

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:45.149
Well, if we look at amoeba, it seems to be
both simple and unclear.

00:11:45.149 --> 00:11:51.259
Sex is a way to create genetic variability,
but it is not the only option.

00:11:51.259 --> 00:11:57.100
Amoebas employ a wide range of sexual and
asexual life cycles, some of which we've been

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:00.020
able to observe and understand.

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:01.930
Some of which we have not.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:07.319
Arthur Guiterman wrote another poem, called
appropriately enough "Sex."

00:12:07.319 --> 00:12:08.579
It begins:

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:23.700
We appreciate that Guiterman was seemingly
as preoccupied with the reproductive habits

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:25.380
of amoebas as we are.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:32.269
Of course, now we know that the science underlying
this stanza might not be quite correct, that

00:12:32.269 --> 00:12:37.410
amoebas were not the beginners of sex but
rather the product of it.

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:44.209
Just like sex itself is not just a driver
of evolution, but a result of it as well.

00:12:44.209 --> 00:12:49.450
And that the loss of sex might also be an
evolutionary strategy.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:54.890
If anything, looking at sex through this lens
seems like a reminder that the amoebas at

00:12:54.890 --> 00:13:05.699
the start--the ones we might desognate "primitive"--are
often much more complex than we think.

00:13:05.699 --> 00:13:11.890
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:17.630
If you’re glad that there’s a channel
on youtube that is able to dive into the weird

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:23.570
mating habits of amoeba, well the people on
screen the folks you want to thank for that.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:25.949
They are our patrons on Patreon.

00:13:25.949 --> 00:13:32.499
And if you want to join them, You can check
us out at patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:13:32.499 --> 00:13:36.870
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James Weiss, check out Jam &amp; Germs

